          "type": "turn",
          "question": "From what age does guidance describe screening for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "Both guideline sources start screening at age 35, but they differ on who should be screened after that.\n\n- **Diabetes association guidance** (National diabetes association standards of care and preventive services guidance) screens all adults from age 35. It also screens younger adults who are overweight or obese and have at least one added risk factor, such as a close relative with diabetes or a history of gestational diabetes. Where a screening test is normal, the usual repeat interval is three years.\n- **Preventive-services guidance** (cited in the same screening document) limits routine screening to adults aged 35 to 70 who are overweight or obese. It bases this on the group in whom screening trials showed benefit.\n\nThe two positions would lead to very different numbers of people being tested, so \"who should be screened\" depends on which guidance you follow. The retrieved passages do not say how strong the evidence is for each position beyond the trial-benefit reasoning noted above, and they do not address screening for adults over 70 in any detail.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. If you are wondering whether you should be tested, a clinician can weigh your age, weight and other risk factors against these recommendations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
